# Data integrity

Nothing is ever lost and the data is never inconsistent (the design's seventh principle). The data layer is the most critical part of jaw.

## What counts as the data layer

Any change that touches, or changes the behaviour of:
- `crates/jawd/src/store/`, `crates/jawd/src/sync*`, `crates/jawd/src/feed/`, `crates/jawd/src/find*`, `crates/jawd/src/send*`, `crates/jawd/src/engine/` (acks to the engine)
- `engine/internal/wa/` and `proto/engine/` (session.db, acks, the spool)
- any SQL, migration, index, trigger, PRAGMA, transaction boundary, ack or file written for the data, wherever it is

`scripts/check-data-audit.py` holds the list of paths; extend it when the data layer grows.

## Rules

Read "jawd › Storage › Data integrity" and "Migrations" in `docs/plans/2026-10-16-jaw-design.md` before changing the data layer, and follow every rule there. The ones broken most easily:
- Nothing is acknowledged before a synced commit (`FULL`, flushed in full: `F_FULLFSYNC` on macOS). `checkpoint_fullfsync` on every connection; `jaw.db`'s go through jaw's VFS (`store/vfs.rs`) with `fullfsync` off, so it flushes a commit's sync in full and fences only a checkpoint's and a new WAL header's, with a barrier on the Mac's own drive.
- Every write is an idempotent upsert with merge rules; replays, duplicates and out-of-order arrivals change nothing twice. Failures are quarantined, never dropped.
- Counters are derived (triggers or recomputed in the same transaction), never adjusted by hand.
- A merged migration is never edited; take the next number when you merge; declare its kind (schema step, data step, backfill) and its tables' class; never drop jaw's own data.
- No `OFFSET`, no scans of large tables, no temporary b-tree sorts, no `ANALYZE`; pin new statements' plans in the plan test.
- Row keys others hold are never reused (`AUTOINCREMENT` where rows are deleted).
- Every file and queue is bounded and reported in `jawd status`.

## The audit, before landing

1. When the gate passes, and before pushing, have the database expert audit the branch: launch the `database-expert` subagent (`.cursor/agents/database-expert.md`; if that type isn't offered, launch a general-purpose subagent told to read that file first and follow it as its brief) with the worktree path and `origin/main..HEAD`. Start a fresh auditor for each round rather than resuming a long-running one. If you can't launch subagents, don't push: report back to whoever started you and ask for the audit.
2. Fix everything it marks as data loss or inconsistency, then have it audit again. It writes `docs/audits/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` ending in `Verdict: approved`.
3. Commit the note in the branch, and give every commit that touches the data layer the trailer `Data-Audit: docs/audits/<file>.md`, for example `git rebase origin/main -x 'git commit --amend --no-edit --trailer "Data-Audit: docs/audits/2026-10-17-sync.md"'`.
4. `make check-audit` must pass locally; the pre-push hook (`make hooks`) refuses a push to main without it, and CI runs it again. Never push with `--no-verify`. Land an approved branch from the worktree it was built in, never from a scratch worktree made for the push, and never from a run that was told to hold.

Never skip, fake or self-write the audit, and never land a data-layer change whose audit isn't `Verdict: approved`.

Never copy the owner's `session.db` anywhere, and never run the Go engine on a folder holding it or a copy: tests on copies of the owner's `jaw.db` use `--engine demo`.

Never stop processes by name (`pkill jawd`, `killall jaw-engine`, `pkill -f jawd`): the owner's jawd may be running. Stop only the PIDs you started, or a process whose command line names your own temp folder.
